would thank you very much in your car in my car all right can the whole family go absolutely seven

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:20.740
seats good we appreciate the uh guiding tour of uh jeffrey and susanna and you three and one that's

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:29.450
great okay and so we started out for our first introduction to the magic of paris

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:34.520
Crosset-sur-Seine is situated along the river to the west of Paris,

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:36.900
so we approached the city from the west,

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:41.640
and here we got our first view of the magnificent Arc de Triomphe,

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:46.800
standing at the western edge of the city like a giant entrance gate.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:54.580
Linda skillfully navigated the huge traffic circle around the arch,

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:59.520
and then we were driving down one of the most famous avenues in all the world.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.230
The Champs-Elysees, heading straight for the heart of Paris.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:09.090
At the end of the avenue stands the Egyptian obelisk,

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:13.310
marking the great square which is now known as the Place de la Concorde.

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:29.970
And then we got our first view of the Eiffel.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:59.360
tower over there and are we crossing the Seine now? Yes, we're crossing the Seine and those gold things on the Pont de Alexandre Troyes. Right here is the National Assembly. And now we're on the Rive Coast. That means the left bank. And down the boulevard Sinister Man. I should look to where I was going. This is the left bank. It's where the Sorbonne is.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:25.930
This is a grandchild's view of the sidewalks of Paris, walking through the left bank, university

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:29.970
section of the city, also known as the old Roman or Latin Quarter.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.820
because in early centuries they spoke Latin here.

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:36.040
It was fun to think of authors like Ernest Hemingway

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:38.800
walking these same sidewalks in the 1920s.

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:41.440
The first thing that I wanted to do

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:44.600
so that Robbins could be a knowledgeable grandfather

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:48.740
was to buy a copy of the weekly Pariscope magazine,

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.840
providing a guide to This Week in Paris.

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:55.380
Our first major point of interest this afternoon

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:58.380
was to be the famous Sorbonne University,

00:12:58.620 --> 00:12:59.980
an early part of which...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.440
was this elaborate Roman-style church built in the 1600s

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:07.860
and housing the remains of the powerful Cardinal Richelieu,

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:09.920
so dramatically fictionalized

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:12.720
in Alexander Dumas' novel, The Three Musketeers.

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:16.460
The Sorbonne, France's most famous university,

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:19.920
had its beginnings in the year 1253,

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:22.240
with only a handful of students

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:24.080
listening to street-corner lectures.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:27.590
But by 1400, it had become the headquarters

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:29.370
for the University of Paris.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.540
and had over 15,000 undergraduates.

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:35.380
The university has had a checkered history,

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:38.300
but over the centuries, the lecture halls

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:41.780
surrounding this courtyard were the academic homes

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:46.040
of such great teachers and students as St. Thomas Aquinas,

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:51.730
Roger Bacon, Dante, Erasmus, Descartes, and John Calvin.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:56.470
Jeffrey had seen on Broadway the musical version

00:13:56.470 --> 00:13:59.370
of Victor Hugo's masterpiece, Les Miserables.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.020
So he especially appreciated this statue memorializing the great French writer.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:12.710
Also in the courtyard is a statue of Louis Pasteur who founded the science of bacteriology

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:17.940
and whose medical discoveries brought about the prevention and control of many previously

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:18.900
fatal diseases.

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:24.840
We could not help thinking here about how many millions of grandchildren around the world

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:29.960
today owe their lives to this great 19th century Frenchman who studied at the Sorbonne.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:38.420
Just down the street from the University has located our main objective for this first day's excursion,

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:45.090
the Musee de Cluny, the medieval Cluny Museum of early artworks and relics

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:48.310
built on the site of the city's ancient Roman baths.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:55.990
Over there where the Roman ruins are, of the baths, they were built around the 2nd century AD.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:59.980
And over here, you can see where the Gothic archies are.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:06.100
actually connects with the Roman band.

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:11.490
What made this site especially attractive

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:13.330
for our younger tour guides

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:16.310
was the availability of an outdoor playground

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:18.250
where they could amuse themselves

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:21.330
while the older generation folks went inside

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:24.610
the museum mansion to view its exquisite treasures.

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:29.470
So leaving Susanna in the care of her older brother, Jeff,

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:36.470
Linda, Meg, and Robbins set out to find the antiquities located indoors.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:44.160
The most famous display at the Clooney Museum is exhibited in a dimly lit, high-ceiling circular room,

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:50.550
around the walls of which are hung a series of magnificent storytelling tapestries,

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:56.470
six of them dating back to the 15th century and known as the Lady and the Unicorn.

00:15:56.980 --> 00:15:59.960
Each of these hand-woven tapestries colorfully...

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:06.780
portrays a richly clad lady in the presence of a white unicorn and a tawny lion. There are

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:11.500
fascinating interpretations of the symbolism involved in these elaborate panels, but five of

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:17.380
them depict the lady demonstrating to the unicorn the five human senses. In this first one,

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:22.960
she's showing the sense of sight by having the bearded unicorn look at its own image in a

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:29.390
handheld mirror. In the second panel, the sense of sound is depicted by the playing of a miniature

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:29.770
your organ.

00:16:30.190 --> 00:16:32.230
with the assistance of a lovely handmaiden.

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:36.240
In the next panel, with a standing unicorn and lion,

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:40.600
the sense of taste is illustrated by the lady taking sweet from a bowl.

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:45.000
The sense of smell is shown by the lady holding a garland of flowers

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.840
against an incredibly rich and detailed background.

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:51.080
Finally, we have the sense of touch,

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:53.890
with a lady reaching her hand out to touch

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:57.230
the long, straight, pointed horn of the mythical unicorn.

00:16:58.120 --> 00:16:59.980
Notice that the horn is depicted...

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:06.800
with small grooves spiraling upward, making it very much like the real-life tusk of a small

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:13.060
species of whale known as the narwhal, which is found only in Arctic waters. And in fact,

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:19.360
displayed on the wall here, above these Clooney tapestries, is an actual six-foot-long narwhal

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:25.700
tusk acquired from some Arctic whale hunter. It is reported that in late medieval times,

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:29.920
narwhal tusks brought back by early Arctic explorers

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:33.260
were claimed to be the horns of unicorns.

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:39.730
In any case, viewing these magnificent hand-woven medieval tapestries at Cluny

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:42.450
is an unforgettable aesthetic experience.

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:49.040
When we got back to the playground, we found that Susanna was anxious to show us

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:52.940
one of the exciting modern-day wonders which she had discovered.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:08.930
Susanna, you like doing that?

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:29.960
After this, what better way could there be to wind up our first day of Parisian...

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:38.540
sightseeing than for grandparents to treat daughter and grandchildren to soft drinks at an outdoor sidewalk cafe near the university.

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:07.410
The next day, Jeffrey and grandfather decided to undertake a joint photographic expedition.

00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:13.870
We started out at the Circular Fountain at the Place de la Concorde with the Eiffel Tower in the background.

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:22.030
This is where, during the French Revolution in 1793, the guillotine was set up which beheaded more than 1,300 victims.

00:19:22.510 --> 00:19:29.030
The obelisk of Luxor was originally erected along the Nile River around 1,300 B.C.

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:29.970
It is separate.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:36.200
76 feet tall, covered with hieroglyphics, and was given to France by Egypt in 1831.

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:40.900
It stands in a direct line between the Louvre and the Arc de Triomphe.

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:46.210
It is a mile-long walk down the Champs-Élysées to the giant arch,

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:50.130
but schoolchildren and retirees seem equally to enjoy it.

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:55.340
The famous Grand Palace along the way was built for the World Exhibition of 1900,

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:59.960
and I was particularly impressed by the monumental sculpture of a chariot.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.760
and four horses charging abreast which covers the rooftop corner.

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:08.210
Geoffrey didn't allow the traffic hazards on the avenue

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:11.430
to interfere with his quest as an expert photographer

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:13.110
for just the right perspective.

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:17.210
The two of us thought it would be fun to try to record on film,

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:20.710
each in our own way, something of the wonder and the glory

00:20:20.710 --> 00:20:24.770
of the Champs-Élysées and the historic arch which crowns it.

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:29.260
Geoff created a remarkable photographic essay of still pictures

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:29.980
and

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:37.020
I took these scenes with my video camera. The Arc de Triomphe is the largest monument of its kind

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:47.020
ever built. This colossal stone archway is 164 feet high, 148 feet long, and 72 feet wide.

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:54.760
It was conceived by Emperor Napoleon Bonaparte in 1806 as a way to honor his soldiers for their

00:20:54.760 --> 00:20:59.890
military victories, telling them that, like ancient Romans, they would go home.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:08.400
beneath triumphal arches. There are four heroic larger-than-life sets of sculpture on the walls

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:15.590
of the main arch columns. The first one here dramatically depicts a call to battle with the

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:26.900
spirit of France urging the soldiers on. The walls inside the archways are inscribed the names of

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:29.960
scores of famous battle sites and military offices.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:39.830
On the left side wall of the main arch, there is a second giant sculpture portraying the crowning of Napoleon as emperor.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:47.300
Napoleon himself was dethroned, exiled, and died before the arch was even half completed.

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:58.660
It was not actually finished until 1836 under King Louis Philippe, at which time it was finally dedicated to the glory of France's armed forces.

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:05.670
As we move now to the south side of the wide pedestrian walkway surrounding the arch,

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:11.800
we can clearly see that the monumental structure is a four-way arch

00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:17.060
with lesser but still massive openings extending through each side column

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:21.260
and with friezes depicting famous battles up above them.

00:22:22.100 --> 00:22:27.020
Experiencing firsthand this incredibly awesome architectural masterpiece

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:29.980
moved me that evening to write...

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:40.250
this poem reflecting on the arches significance majestic strong and powerful it stands implanted at

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:48.720
the head of double-breasted boulevards a monumental tribute to the dead the victories it celebrates

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:56.740
alas are those achieved in war where thousands upon thousands killed each other ordered by an

00:22:56.740 --> 00:22:59.890
emperor and in this latest century

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.600
to halt the Nazi tyranny of millions slaughtered,

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:07.820
brave men died so all could live again in liberty.

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:15.050
This towering arc gives honor to one kind of glory from the past.

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:19.220
In future years, may triumphs rise

00:23:19.220 --> 00:23:24.000
from heroes' deeds achieved in peace at last.

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:40.340
Throughout the day in the open spaces between the lofty pillars,

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:46.410
people of all ages congregate, overawed by such towering majesty around them.

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:50.030
The names of people and places carved upon the walls

00:23:50.030 --> 00:23:54.490
are largely commemorative of military exploits from the Napoleonic era.

00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:59.210
Under the pavement between the columns on the east side, however,

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:06.840
There has been added in the 20th century the tomb of an unknown French soldier from World War I,

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:13.860
marked with an eternal flame, and the inscription,

00:24:15.230 --> 00:24:23.700
Here reposes a soldier of France who died for his country, 1914-1918.

00:24:38.030 --> 00:24:54.580
Many people do not realize it, but inside the main columns of the arch, there are a circular staircase and an elevator by which observers can ascend to the top of the monument, where one can walk around the rectangular perimeter of the roof and peer out on all sides.

00:24:54.700 --> 00:24:59.020
The views of Paris from this vantage point are fantastic.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:24.230
great photographic opportunities for grandson Jeff. Looking to the east down

00:25:24.230 --> 00:25:29.900
the broad avenue of Champs-Élysées toward the old part of the city one can see in the

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:36.590
distance, the Egyptian obelisk at the Place de la Concorde, with the Louvre spreading out behind it.

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:43.400
On the right can be dimly discerned the twin square towers and pointed steeple of the Notre-Dame

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:50.830
Cathedral. The long sloping hilltop on which the Arc de Triomphe was erected is known as

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:58.720
L'Etoile, the star, because from it as center, 12 major avenues radiate out in all directions.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:06.040
south, one avenue leads across the Seine to Paris's other sky-filling landmark, the Eiffel Tower.

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:14.870
Most of these radiating roadways were laid out in 1854 by Napoleon III's radical city planner,

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:21.220
Baron Haussmann, who ruthlessly tore down all the existing housing and other structures that stood

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:29.980
in the way of his grand 12-point star design. On the opposite side of the arch from the Champs-Élysées,

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:37.410
one follows the avenue to Charles de Gaulle westward to the remarkable new urban development

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:43.920
known as La Défense with a giant modern aluminum arch surrounded by contemporary glass and steel

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:50.890
high-rise buildings. Continuing our panoramic circle, we can see what an extensive metropolitan

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:57.610
area of Paris encompasses. Barely visible on the north horizon is the Sacre Coeur Basilica

00:26:57.610 --> 00:26:59.830
topping the famous artist district.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.420
known as Montmartre.

00:27:03.470 --> 00:27:05.090
Back now to our starting point,

00:27:05.350 --> 00:27:07.330
all the way down the Champs-Élysées

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:09.550
is another triumphal arch

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:11.940
known as the Arc du Carousel

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:13.960
with a four-horse chariot

00:27:13.960 --> 00:27:15.000
mounted on its top.

00:27:15.660 --> 00:27:17.080
This much smaller arch,

00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:18.460
also ordered by Napoleon,

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:20.640
stands in front of the courtyard

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:22.520
of the greatest and most famous

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:25.160
museum of art anywhere in the world,

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:26.190
the Louvre.

00:27:26.780 --> 00:27:28.420
The open courtyard of the Louvre

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:29.900
is surrounded on three sides

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:29.980
Bye.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:35.540
by palatial buildings added one after the other over the centuries to make a giant rectangular

00:27:35.540 --> 00:27:42.070
horseshoe. As one gazes in awe at these ornate stone-blocked structures from the past, one is

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:48.770
startled to find in the very center of the courtyard a striking 70-foot-tall pyramid built

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:56.750
of glass, steel tubes, and cables. Designed by the famous Chinese-American architect I. M. Pai,

00:27:56.750 --> 00:27:58.970
It was installed in 1989.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:04.900
nearly 200 years after the opening to the public of this historic royal museum.

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:08.590
The pyramid now serves as the main entrance to the museum,

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:13.830
providing a gigantic skylight over the spacious, modernistic, new underground lobby

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:16.770
where there are ticket offices, shops, and eating facilities.

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:22.460
From the glass-protected surface opening, we descend to the lobby on escalators

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:26.000
to start the tour of multiple rooms and hallways

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:29.720
where Grandma and Grandpa were able to see in one afternoon.

00:28:30.250 --> 00:28:36.430
more world famous original art masterpieces than we had seen collectively before throughout our

00:28:36.430 --> 00:28:48.450
entire lives we can only include a few samples here but perhaps the western world's most famous

00:28:48.450 --> 00:28:55.580
sculpture is the armless venus de milo carved in marble around the first century before christ

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:59.740
it sets in stone an ageless ideal of the female form.

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:06.080
Almost equally famous is the winged victory of Samothrace,

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:09.880
discovered in 1863 on an island in the Aegean Sea.

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:12.920
Dating back to 200 years before Christ,

00:29:13.170 --> 00:29:16.810
this headless marble masterpiece may have stood on the bow of a ship.

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:20.320
So realistically does the tunic cling to her body,

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:22.900
we can almost feel the wind and spray.

00:29:27.180 --> 00:29:29.970
Lisa, painted by the 16th century,

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:35.660
century italian artist leonardo da vinci has been called the most famous painting in the world

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:41.560
is a portrait of the wife of a florentine nobleman the mystical setting in the almost

00:29:41.560 --> 00:29:48.240
imperceptible smile of her delicate mouth and self-confident eyes lend an aura of eternal

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:59.910
mystery to this enigmatic woman this is a powerful sculpture by the italian renaissance master michel

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:07.260
called the bound slave. It profoundly portrays his suffering while struggling to free himself

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:13.670
from the tight bonds. Does it symbolically represent the human soul enchained by the

00:30:13.670 --> 00:30:20.290
weight of the human body? Moving now to the Holland section, here we have a self-portrait

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:25.570
by perhaps the greatest of the 17th century Dutch artist Rembrandt. He made many studies

00:30:25.570 --> 00:30:29.830
of his own face, seeking to understand how its features reflect.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:36.020
moods. This searching portrayal at the age of 54 shows Rembrandt's mastery of color,

00:30:36.280 --> 00:30:43.460
radiance, and shadow at the height of his career. Another 17th century Dutch artist exhibited

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:49.720
at the Louvre is Vermeer. This painting, called The Astronomer, shows Vermeer's remarkable

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:55.540
ability to capture the play of light from the window on various surfaces. There is harmony

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:59.960
and richness, a quiet beauty and dignity, raising this work to...

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:09.400
In 18th century France, many court artists emphasize the sensuous beauty of the female body.

00:31:10.510 --> 00:31:15.590
The soft color and texture of the skin are remarkable in this painting by Boucher,

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:20.150
showing the huntress Diana getting out of her bath with the assistance of a handmaiden.

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:29.740
Finally, representing 19th century revolutionary France, we have this large dramatic mural by Delacroix.

00:31:30.250 --> 00:31:59.620
entitled liberty leading the people it reminds us of the student-led revolt in les miserables with delacroix himself wearing a top hat and brandishing a gun following liberty over the fallen bodies of his comrades it was raining when we finally emerged from the

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:05.880
maze of the Louvre adjacent to the Seine River and Meg and Linda headed for

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:11.000
the Metro subway to get us back to the Barstow family temporary residence in

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:16.000
Croce-sur-Seine. The next day was to be a momentous one when we would be joined

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:18.160
by three other family members.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:36.200
This whole extra week here was marvelous, but now it's time to hold family together,

00:32:36.400 --> 00:32:40.040
and we've arranged to meet Dan and Eva and Cedar.

00:32:40.220 --> 00:32:40.420
Where?

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:42.440
At the Eiffel Tower.

00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:43.500
The Eiffel Tower?

00:32:43.820 --> 00:32:44.660
Oh, my goodness.

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:46.480
Off to the Eiffel Tower, and we'll bring them back.

00:32:46.640 --> 00:32:47.420
We'll see you later.

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:58.450
Between the Arc de Triomphe and the Eiffel Tower on the north bank of the Seine,

00:32:58.590 --> 00:32:59.970
there's some spectacular...

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:04.220
fountains on the grounds of the monumental museum complex known as the

00:33:04.220 --> 00:33:09.370
Palais de Chalot. Looking south from the palace's broad terrace, one is rewarded

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:18.730
with a superb view of the Eiffel Tower in its fantastic entirety. Extending over

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:24.770
1,000 feet up into the heavens, this incredibly awesome structure was erected

00:33:24.770 --> 00:33:29.670
over 100 years ago in 1889 to commemorate the 100th anniversary

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:36.390
the French Revolution. It was designed by the master French engineer Gustave Eiffel, and for

00:33:36.390 --> 00:33:41.790
40 years after it was built, it remained the tallest man-made structure on earth, not being

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:47.490
surpassed until 1930 with the Chrysler Building in New York City. There are three built-in platforms,

00:33:47.690 --> 00:33:55.260
a large lower one, a middle one, and a distant top one, for observers to enjoy. Radio and television

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:57.730
equipment now cover the top of the tower.

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:06.040
Still awed by the sight of this overpowering edifice and eager to examine it more closely,

00:34:07.400 --> 00:34:12.380
Robbins and Meg headed across the bridge over the seine toward the base of the tower,

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:16.730
looking for the newly arrived three other members of our family.

00:34:17.720 --> 00:34:23.590
And sure enough, there they were, ready to greet us with open arms and excited spirits.

00:34:24.090 --> 00:34:29.970
On the right, our daughter, Cedar Barstow from Boulder, Colorado, and on the left, our

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:33.980
younger son, Dan, and his wife, Eva, who had flown in from New Britain, Connecticut.

00:34:46.390 --> 00:34:55.040
As we approached the tower, we were tremendously impressed by the revolutionary exposed way

00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:59.980
in which Eiffel had joined massive beams of structural steel and pig...

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:06.400
iron lattice work to create this colossal, symmetrical, all-metal skyscraper. He anchored

00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:13.360
it on four giant legs, spaced equally apart, making it so stable that even in the strongest

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:19.620
winds, the top does not sway more than four and a half inches. The total weight of the

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:28.950
metal tower is 7,000 tons. Before making our ascent, we stopped beneath the overarching framework

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:29.970
to eat

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:36.040
a picnic lunch sharing bits of it with the panhandling pigeons that seemed to be everywhere

00:35:53.880 --> 00:35:59.640
the entrance to the tower is through a gateway in the east pillar

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:07.220
which hosts a slanting elevator used to transport visitors up and down inside the mammoth leg.

00:36:08.210 --> 00:36:11.570
This is the first of two elevator systems within the tower,

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:16.210
and we crowded into this one to be taken up to the first and second platforms

00:36:16.210 --> 00:36:18.210
which encircle the core of the structure.

00:36:19.420 --> 00:36:24.280
This is a dramatic ride with tantalizing glimpses of the disappearing ground below.

00:36:48.510 --> 00:36:57.920
When we got to the second level, we emerged onto the open platform, crowded with tourists,

00:36:58.500 --> 00:36:59.980
literally from all over the world.

00:37:00.030 --> 00:37:06.210
and tried to find a spot by the railing for our first panoramic view of Paris

00:37:06.360 --> 00:37:09.700
spread out on all sides before us.

00:37:17.800 --> 00:37:29.980
The second elevator system provides a vertical lift from the middle-level platform all the way to the...

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:37.460
top inside the central shaft of the tower it presented an awesome prospect but this is what

00:37:37.460 --> 00:37:48.440
we had come for we had to wait our turn to enter the elevator cab and when we finally started up

00:37:48.440 --> 00:37:55.400
the ride seemed to last forever but it was an incomparable thrill to watch the girders

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:59.980
pass by the window and to look out at the surrounding area from a constant

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:29.680
increasing height the platform at the top has two levels the lower one

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:36.870
closed by protective glass panels. The upper level is open to the sky. We wanted to absorb

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:42.330
the view and take photographs first from the lower enclosed level, where we didn't have

00:38:42.330 --> 00:38:51.260
to worry about the wind and fenced-in railings. Using telephoto viewfinders, we tried to locate

00:38:51.260 --> 00:38:59.970
recognizable landmarks, such as the Notre Dame Cathedral. Looking eastward over the

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:05.280
the rooftops toward the older central part of Paris we could see emerging from

00:39:05.280 --> 00:39:10.680
the heart of the city the dividing waterway of the Seine River it runs

00:39:10.680 --> 00:39:14.680
right through the middle of Paris and there are almost too many bridges to

00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:21.230
count linking the right and left banks the Eiffel Tower is situated on the south

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:26.440
side of the river and the famous Champs-Élysées and Arc de Triomphe are on

00:39:26.440 --> 00:39:29.980
the north side from this distance the triumphal

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:43.950
arch seems almost like a miniature model moving to the open upper level of the top deck we get

00:39:43.950 --> 00:39:50.230
a close-up view of the maze of weather radio and television equipment surmounting the top

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:57.130
and we gain an even broader view of the surrounding environs.

00:40:01.020 --> 00:40:10.630
moving our sights from the Arc de Triomphe down to the river slowly drifting by below us and the

00:40:10.630 --> 00:40:16.210
bridge crossing it at the foot of the tower, we now could appreciate from a different perspective

00:40:16.210 --> 00:40:21.890
the beauty of the gardens and fountains we had seen earlier beneath the terrace of the Palais de

00:40:21.890 --> 00:40:29.980
Shiloh. They give life and grace to the grounds of this.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:42.650
extensive museum complex which dominates the hillside above the river still crossed by many

00:40:42.770 --> 00:40:52.260
bridges the seine wends its way westward into the also crowded but less populous suburbs of paris

00:40:57.590 --> 00:40:59.930
Right below the tower on the south side

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:08.290
is the Champs-de-Mars, an all-open grassy park providing welcome relief from the surrounding

00:41:08.290 --> 00:41:15.460
masses of buildings. Using binoculars and taking countless photos, we spent most of the afternoon

00:41:15.460 --> 00:41:23.320
looking out over this unparalleled vista, alternately shadowed by clouds and illuminated by sunlight

00:41:23.320 --> 00:41:25.460
shining through breaks in the clouds.

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:37.880
Before leaving the top, our attention was called to the parade of large, elongated, river-going boats,

00:41:38.040 --> 00:41:42.500
which we could see constantly plying the seine with sightseeing passengers.

00:41:43.620 --> 00:41:46.620
That, we thought, should be our next excursion.

00:41:48.070 --> 00:41:53.690
Finally, we all descended in the lift to the second-level platform

00:41:53.690 --> 00:41:58.360
and then decided to walk down the rest of the way on the iron stairways.

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:21.930
The Eiffel Tower had to have been the high point of this trip.

00:42:22.680 --> 00:42:29.970
The next day, we did indeed go on a boat excursion on one of the famous bateaux mouches, which carry hundreds of passengers.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:35.060
every day for two-hour rides up and down the seine this time our granddaughter

00:42:35.060 --> 00:42:41.220
Susanna served as our family tour leader escorting grandpa down the gangway and

00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:48.870
then up the stairs to the open upper deck where we could get a much better view

00:42:49.980 --> 00:42:54.650
these motor driven boats are built just for this purpose and depart every

00:42:54.650 --> 00:42:59.600
half hour from a loading platform in clear sight of the Eiffel

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:07.800
Linda had prepared a delicious chicken lunch for us to eat on board.

00:43:32.310 --> 00:43:46.000
As the boat pulled out and moved slowly along the river,

00:43:46.390 --> 00:43:49.510
we were given a running commentary in both French and English.

00:44:20.530 --> 00:44:28.740
Heading east, parallel to the Champs-Elysees, we pass numerous bridges with a wide variety of styles and sculptures.

00:44:32.560 --> 00:44:59.950
before long on the left we can see the obelisk at la place de la concorde with former royal palaces in the background then on the right we catch a brief glimpse of the french national assembly building far beyond this also on the right is the famous musee

00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:07.530
d'Orsay originally built as a railway station but now transformed into an outstanding art museum

00:45:07.720 --> 00:45:18.110
especially noted for its exhibits of 19th century impressionist painters as we pass the outside of

00:45:18.110 --> 00:45:23.660
the louvre over to our left scarcely recognizable from this viewpoint we come to the part of the

00:45:23.660 --> 00:45:29.200
river where long stone promenades have been constructed along the edge of the water

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:35.860
These romantic walkways have long been popular with lovers, children, and artists,

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:39.150
and they have also been a favorite setting for films.

00:45:40.690 --> 00:45:44.050
Now we come to the ancient heart of the city,

00:45:45.300 --> 00:45:50.200
and we encounter the first of two large islands right in the middle of the river.

00:45:51.020 --> 00:45:53.000
The boat takes the branch to the right,

00:45:53.960 --> 00:45:59.520
and we pass the equestrian statue of the famous 17th century French king,

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:07.080
louis the fourteenth known as the sun king students and tourists alike frequent both banks

00:46:07.080 --> 00:46:13.850
of the river here where the walls are lined with book stalls and local art displays the old sorbonne

00:46:13.850 --> 00:46:19.000
university is not far from the river's edge and the various bridges that cross to the heavily

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:26.620
built up islands the foremost island the isle de la cité is the site of many historic buildings

00:46:26.620 --> 00:46:29.930
and it is here on this age

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:35.900
ancient island that we find one of the world's most famous cathedrals, the Notre-Dame de

00:46:35.900 --> 00:46:43.280
Paris. Construction of this architectural masterpiece with its two massive square towers was begun

00:46:43.280 --> 00:46:51.700
in 1163 and completed nearly 200 years later in 1345. For over 600 years it has been a

00:46:51.700 --> 00:46:56.960
central part of French history and religion. Located on a mid-island site used in earlier

00:46:56.960 --> 00:46:59.960
centuries by Gauls and Romans, the Notre Dame

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:06.540
Madame Cathedral combines beauty and strength to give honor and glory to Our Lady of Paris,

00:47:06.940 --> 00:47:12.680
the Mother of Christ. This statue of the Madonna and Child, dating from the 14th century, is the

00:47:12.680 --> 00:47:18.700
best known of the 37 representations of Mary found within the church and clearly demonstrates

00:47:18.700 --> 00:47:26.220
the veneration which marks all of the cathedral's great works of art. During hundreds of tumultuous

00:47:26.220 --> 00:47:29.900
years the original building is undergoing changes in restoration

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:39.960
But it remains basically the same, except for the addition of the tall pointed spire over the chancel roof in the middle of the 19th century.

00:47:41.470 --> 00:47:51.550
Among the church's most famous features are three giant circular stained glass windows, known as the roses, high up on the front and side walls.

00:47:52.360 --> 00:47:59.920
Structurally, each one is perfectly patterned with brightly colored panes of glass radiating out like a sun.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:07.890
burst, supported by a symmetrical stone framework. Only one of the windows now contains the original

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:14.230
colored glass used when first erected, but their construction required tall, thin walls for the

00:48:14.230 --> 00:48:20.050
cathedral. Looking skyward out through one of these windows from the inside, one feels an aura

00:48:20.050 --> 00:48:22.740
of ethereal magnificence.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:36.240
The clear tower on the left, here undergoing repairs, houses the mammoth 13-ton bell which

00:48:36.240 --> 00:48:41.240
Quasimodo rang in Victor Hugo's epic novel The Hunchback of Notre Dame.

00:48:42.300 --> 00:48:47.720
Viewing the interior of the church later in the day, we were struck almost with unbelief

00:48:47.720 --> 00:48:53.900
at the towering height of the Gothic arches vaulting over the long central nave and chancel.

00:48:53.900 --> 00:48:59.510
The galleries on each side are upheld by rows of majestic columns.

00:49:00.380 --> 00:49:07.780
giving the cathedral a seating capacity of nearly ten thousand for mass and organ recitals as our

00:49:07.780 --> 00:49:14.260
boat passes the south side we can see the flying buttresses used to support the tall thin walls

00:49:14.260 --> 00:49:21.940
from the outside a fantastic architectural innovation of the middle ages from the east

00:49:21.940 --> 00:49:27.580
tip of the island notre dame seems almost to float like a vast stone ship above the seine

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:36.620
Continuing our boat trip now around the Ile de la Cité on the north side, we head back

00:49:36.620 --> 00:49:42.300
past our original starting point to view the Eiffel Tower from a different perspective.

00:49:43.090 --> 00:49:48.360
Further along the river, there is a remarkable reproduction of the Statue of Liberty, much

00:49:48.360 --> 00:49:54.240
smaller than the New York Harbor Monument given to the United States by France in 1886.

00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:59.360
But here together are the images of two of the most famous symbols in the world

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:02.560
linking our two countries in common cause.

00:50:05.130 --> 00:50:07.930
And so our tour of the Seine is concluded,

00:50:07.930 --> 00:50:12.090
and we have had a truly awesome family excursion.

00:50:15.040 --> 00:50:18.960
On the following day, it was grandson Jeffrey's turn

00:50:18.960 --> 00:50:21.620
to take us for some final city sightseeing,

00:50:21.860 --> 00:50:27.580
starting with the indescribably ornate and opulent Grand Opera House of Paris,

00:50:28.070 --> 00:50:29.870
designed by Charles Garnier.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:37.440
it was opened in 1875, a monumental mixture of competing styles of architecture and sculpture.

00:50:37.780 --> 00:50:43.760
Geoffrey appears almost dwarfed by the tall arches and pillars covering the building's facade.

00:50:46.260 --> 00:50:53.900
This exterior circle of joyously disporting naked dancers shocked some 19th century sensibilities,

00:50:54.120 --> 00:50:59.980
but it was just one of the flamboyantly multifarious works of art displayed throughout the gigantic

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:06.840
showplace. The broad marble staircase in the foyer branches out into two great

00:51:06.860 --> 00:51:12.120
sets of upper stairs and balustrades leading to the main hallways on the

00:51:12.120 --> 00:51:21.120
second floor. The lighted ceiling high overhead is adorned with colorful

00:51:21.120 --> 00:51:27.270
metaphorical murals making this Parisian opera house a stunning hybrid of palace

00:51:27.270 --> 00:51:28.270
and museum.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:35.460
Behind the curtain in the auditorium is one of the world's largest stages, with room for

00:51:35.460 --> 00:51:37.780
up to 450 performers.

00:51:38.720 --> 00:51:47.060
The hall is surrounded by five tiers of luxurious red and gold lodges, but the auditorium actually

00:51:47.060 --> 00:51:50.020
seats only a little more than 2,000 people.

00:51:52.740 --> 00:51:59.980
Beneath the Chagall-painted ceiling hangs the six-ton chandelier made famous by the

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:07.950
the book and musical the phantom of the opera outside the opera house we found a sidewalk

00:52:07.950 --> 00:52:29.200
organ grinder the final stopping place was very unusual

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:38.110
Through this fancy Forum de Halles gateway, we went down into a giant underground mall in what is called the belly of Paris.

00:52:38.350 --> 00:52:44.920
Here we found perhaps the world's most unique aquarium, the Parc Oceanique Cousin.

00:52:45.000 --> 00:52:49.000
conceived by the famous French explorer Jacques Cousteau.

00:52:49.420 --> 00:52:53.420
It's a waterless showcase for the underwater world

00:52:53.420 --> 00:52:55.340
of our ocean-covered planet.

00:52:55.840 --> 00:52:59.660
Opened in 1989, it takes visitors through three-dimensional...

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:04.000
with models and films of all kinds of sea life.

00:53:37.388 --> 00:53:39.408
This is the strangely beat

00:53:39.820 --> 00:53:41.900
Amazon River Dolphin.

00:53:47.788 --> 00:53:53.068
Instead of viewing fish in tanks and captive dolphins in restraining pools,

00:53:53.324 --> 00:53:55.624
We experience the world of marine mammals.

00:53:55.980 --> 00:53:59.980
through moving visual close-ups of these animals as they truly are.

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:07.610
free in their ocean homes. The most memorable feature of Cousteau's Parisian

00:54:07.610 --> 00:54:13.470
Aquatic Park involves the nomadic blue whale, the largest animal ever to have

00:54:13.470 --> 00:54:14.510
lived on our planet.

00:54:15.220 --> 00:54:29.460
as shown in this wall display it has a heart as large as a small car and on exhibit in this underground hall is a truly awesome life-size model of a female blue whale

00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:29.640
tail.

00:54:30.028 --> 00:54:33.348
33 meters long, over 100 feet.

00:54:56.108 --> 00:54:59.888
this model has been constructed in such a way that through and

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:05.540
opening on the side of its body, a person can walk right up to its giant red heart.

00:55:06.332 --> 00:55:09.992
and then proceed down a passageway inside the whale.

00:55:10.652 --> 00:55:12.652
underneath its mammoth backbone.

00:55:13.372 --> 00:55:14.652
and past its

00:55:14.812 --> 00:55:15.532
Longs.

00:55:16.188 --> 00:55:18.188
and other organs.

00:55:18.716 --> 00:55:26.176
On the right side is a model of a fetus, a life-size, amazingly large, unborn calf.

00:55:26.396 --> 00:55:28.396
still inside its mother's

00:55:28.412 --> 00:55:28.732
body.

00:55:31.210 --> 00:55:34.610
Going through here is an impressive experience.

00:55:35.150 --> 00:55:43.360
Our Paris touring now completed.

00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:51.120
barstow family gathered at a beautiful country manor outside paris for a weekend celebration

00:55:51.120 --> 00:55:57.850
of robin's and meg's 50th wedding anniversary with a scrumptious feast fit for a king and

00:55:57.850 --> 00:55:59.900
queen daughter-in-law eva

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:10.590
and son Daniel on the left side of the table, daughter Cedar, other son David.

00:56:15.000 --> 00:56:29.230
granddaughter Susanna, daughter-in-law Linda, and on the other side of Grandma Meg.

00:56:32.170 --> 00:56:40.140
grandson, Geoffrey, all joined in counting the infinite blessings which half a century

00:56:40.140 --> 00:56:43.700
of mutual caring and sharing had granted us.

00:56:47.220 --> 00:56:56.560
On our last night before leaving the City of Lights, we took a farewell drive under a full moon.
